# THIS FILE DESCRIBES THE CONTENT OF THE LIBRARY
# Lines starting with "!" are machine-readable declarations.

! TEXT_ENCODING utf-8
! TEXT_NORMALIZATION NFC

! ARCHIVE_TYPE BDEA
! VERSION 1.0
! LIBRARY Music

! NAME_ENCODING utf-8
! NAME_NORMALIZATION NFC
! NAME_FORBIDDEN_CHARACTERS / \ : * ? " < > |

! CONTENT_HASH_ALGORITHM md5
! CONTENT_HASH_ENCODING base32
! CONTENT_HASH_SCOPE file-content

#  TYPES

! TYPE dir_id uint32
! TYPE last_dir_id uint32
! TYPE new_dir_id uint32
! TYPE parent_dir_id uint32
! TYPE new_parent_dir_id uint32
! TYPE file_id uint64
! TYPE target_file_id uint64
! TYPE size uint64
! TYPE dir_name string max=120
! TYPE file_name string max=120
! TYPE new_dir_name string max=120
! TYPE new_file_name string max=120
! TYPE hash string max=32
! TYPE extra string max=256
! TYPE volume_id uint32
! TYPE target_volume_id uint32
! TYPE volume_label string max=120
! TYPE date_time string max=15 YYYY-MM-DD_hhmm
! TYPE total_vol_files uint64
! TYPE vol_files_size uint64
! TYPE volume_hash string max=32
! TYPE file_key volume_id file_id

#  SCHEMA

#  Volume boundary
# VOLUME_START opens a volume's section, VOLUME_END closes it.
# volume_id identifies the volume inside the library.
# volume_id is an internal numeric identifier.
# volume_label is the text printed or written on the physical archival medium.
# date_time on VOLUME_START/VOLUME_END is when the index record itself was
# generated, not the physical medium's write timing. Producing one volume's
# index entries is a single fast step, so date_time is the same value
# on VOLUME_START, VOLUME_END, and any AF/XF/XD/RD/RF records within that
# volume's section.
# VOLUME_END repeats volume_label/date_time for corruption detection, then adds:
# total_vol_files/vol_files_size = this volume's own F record count and byte
# size (the only per-volume tallies kept; AF/XF/XD/RD/RF/S have no dedicated
# counter of their own) — not the library's active-view totals, which need
# the whole AF/XF/XD/RD/RF history resolved to compute.
# last_dir_id = highest dir_id assigned in the library as of this volume.
# volume_hash = checksum of just this volume's own F records, derived from
# their hash values already recorded above (concatenate them, no separator
# needed since they're all the same fixed length; hash
# with CONTENT_HASH_ALGORITHM; encode with CONTENT_HASH_ENCODING) rather than by
# re-reading any file's content. Unlike last_dir_id, this is per-volume,
# not a whole-library checkpoint.
! RECORD VOLUME_START volume_id volume_label date_time
! RECORD VOLUME_END volume_id volume_label date_time total_vol_files vol_files_size volume_hash last_dir_id

# A field name ending in "?" is optional and trailing: a data line may omit it
# (one fewer pipe-delimited field than declared); a missing one is empty/absent.

#  Directory
# A logical directory in the library's namespace.
# Directories form a single, stable, append-only namespace tree for the whole
# library.
# dir_id is global across the whole library, assigned once per directory.
# dir_id values start at 1; the library's root is implicit (0) and has no D
# record of its own.
# A (parent_dir_id, dir_name) pair is permanently unique across the library's
# whole history — even a directory emptied by XD keeps occupying its pair.
# Recovering the same logical folder reuses its existing dir_id instead of
# minting a new one.
# Empty directories may be omitted.
# extra is optional/trailing, free-form (e.g. tags).
! RECORD D dir_id parent_dir_id dir_name extra?

#  File
# The file identifier is unique only within the volume.
# The full file identity is defined as (volume_id, file_id).
# volume_id is derived from the enclosing VOLUME_START/VOLUME_END section.
# File identity is defined by content size and content hash.
# file_id is unique only within the volume_id scope.
# dir_id is the containing directory's global id; 0 means the file lives
# directly in the library's root.
# extra is optional/trailing, free-form (e.g. tags).
! RECORD F file_id dir_id file_name size hash extra?

#  Symlink
# S is an alias: a new (dir_id, file_name) tree position that reaches for an
# existing F record's content instead of archiving another physical copy.
# file_id is drawn from the same per-volume counter as F's file_id (shared
# numbering), so XF/RF can correct an S the same way they correct an F.
# target_volume_id/target_file_id may point at an F on any volume; resolved
# through AF/XF/XD like any other content, not a rigid pointer. RF corrections
# to the target's own file_name/dir_id don't affect the alias, which addresses
# it by file_key, not by file_name.
# dir_id may also be 0, same root meaning as F's dir_id.
! RECORD S file_id dir_id file_name target_volume_id target_file_id

#  Active File
# AF marks one physical file record as the active representation of the archived
# content. It may reference a file stored on any volume.
# AF always names an F record, never an S — an alias holds no content of its own
# to be canonical for.
# Whenever the same content exists as more than one F record (any number of
# volumes), AF names exactly one as canonical; the rest drop out of the
# library's active file count without needing XF.
# Like S, this is for libraries that try to avoid duplicate physical copies;
# both AF and S are optional.
! RECORD AF volume_id file_id date_time?

#  Delete File
# XF marks one physical file or alias record as unavailable or excluded from
# the active view of the library. It may reference a file stored on any volume.
! RECORD XF volume_id file_id date_time?

#  Delete Directory
# XD marks a directory, and everything beneath it in the tree (any volume),
# as excluded from the active view. dir_id alone identifies the target,
# since dir_id is never reused. Doesn't retire dir_id: later volumes may still
# file new records under it — and since (parent_dir_id, dir_name) stays
# permanently claimed, recovering the same folder reuses this same dir_id.
# Any such new records must appear after this XD within the same section:
# XD only wipes what's filed under dir_id as of its own position in the log.
# Pure shorthand for writing one XF per file in the subtree; it has no other
# purpose.
! RECORD XD dir_id date_time?

#  Rename/Relocate Directory
# RD corrects an existing directory's parent_dir_id and/or dir_name going
# forward, without writing any new D/F/S record. Because the tree is built
# from dir_id/parent_dir_id links, one RD moves or renames the whole subtree at
# once. The latest RD for a given dir_id wins.
! RECORD RD dir_id new_parent_dir_id new_dir_name date_time?

#  Rename/Relocate File
# RF corrects an existing F or S record's dir_id and/or file_name going
# forward, without rewriting its content. Addresses either an F or an S, like
# AF/XF. Unrelated to AF: AF picks which duplicate is canonical, RF fixes
# where one record sits in the tree. The latest RF for a given
# (volume_id, file_id) wins.
! RECORD RF volume_id file_id new_dir_id new_file_name date_time?


